WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.462
This retention query starts from the recent-activity view.

00:00:03.462 --> 00:00:07.182
A CTE finds the last transaction per customer.

00:00:07.182 --> 00:00:11.667
Another CTE applies a sixty-day inactivity threshold.

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:15.227
That threshold is intentionally report-specific.

00:00:15.227 --> 00:00:21.037
If another report wants ninety days, the shared activity view does not need to change.

00:00:21.037 --> 00:00:27.562
Connect this idea to a data platform materializing expensive summaries for faster consumption.

00:00:27.562 --> 00:00:33.262
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:33.262 --> 00:00:41.002
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:41.002 --> 00:00:46.597
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:46.597 --> 00:00:52.480
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
